== control: is the `[control]` prefix load-bearing? ==
comparator = verify_m3_acceptance.py's own regexes (^ok\s, ^FAIL.*$),
the same two the phase-10.1 arm uses on scripts/test.sh's log.

-- 0. does the comparator discriminate at all? (it must be able to fire) --
   synthetic two-line text -> ^ok\s=1, ^FAIL=1  (expect 1/1)

-- green log (scripts/test.sh exit=0) --
   as written    : ^ok\s= 56  ^FAIL=  0  -> 10.1 PASS
   prefix stripped: ^ok\s= 56  ^FAIL=  0  -> 10.1 PASS
   delta          : ^ok\s +0  ^FAIL +0

-- red log (mutation 1: chmod -x) --
   as written    : ^ok\s=  0  ^FAIL=  0  -> 10.1 FAIL
   prefix stripped: ^ok\s=  0  ^FAIL=  9  -> 10.1 FAIL
   delta          : ^ok\s +0  ^FAIL +9

note: the green-log arm is a NEGATIVE result -- stripping the prefix there
      changes nothing, because a green run of this check writes no `FAIL`
      line and no `ok` line at all. Reporting only that arm would have been
      an empty comparator (0 delta either way). The red-log arm is the one
      with discriminating power, and it is why the prefix is stated in the
      file header as a requirement rather than as a style choice.
